"CSS Properties and Values API Level 1 (https://drafts.css-houdini.org/css-properties-values-api-1/)\nciteturn3070view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3066view0\",\"lineno\":226}); Total lines: 1339\nL201: Note: This implies that a list of values, such as `<color>+` or `<color>#`, will interpolate as a simple list, matching up each component index-by-index, and failing if the number of components doesn’t match.\nL202: \nL203: As an exception to the above rule, a value that parsed as a `<transform-list>`, a `<transform-function>`, or a `<transform-function>+` instead interpolates as per the 【109†transform†drafts.csswg.org】 property.\nL204: Note: If, for whatever reason, a custom property is defined with a syntax of `<transform-function>#`, this will thus first interpolate as a simple list, and then each list item will interpolate as a 【109†transform†drafts.csswg.org】 value.\nL205: \nL206: Note: Registering (or changing the registration) of a custom property can change its computed value, which can start or interrupt a CSS transition.\nL207: ### 2.6. Conditional Rules\nL208: \nL209: As stated in 【23†§ 2.2 Parse-Time Behavior】, both unregistered and 【81†registered】 【77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org】 accept (almost) all possible values at parse-time. 【81†Registered】 【77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org】 only apply their syntax at 【95†computed value†drafts.csswg.org】 time.\nL210: So, all 【77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org】, regardless of whether they’re 【81†registered】 or unregistered, will test as \"true\" in an 【110†@supports†drafts.csswg.org】 rule, so long as you don’t violate the (very liberal) generic syntax for 【77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org】.\nL211: For example, even if a custom property is registered with `syntax: \"<color>\";`, a rule like `@supports (--foo: 1em) {...}` will still evaluate as true and apply those styles, because the declaration does successfully parse as a valid property.\nL212: ### 2.7. Substitution via 【89†var()†drafts.csswg.org】\nL213: \nL214: Like unregistered custom properties, the value of a registered custom property can be substituted into another value with the 【89†var()†drafts.csswg.org】 function. However, registered custom properties substitute as their 【25†computed value】, rather than the original token sequence used to produce that value.\nL215: Any 【89†var()†drafts.csswg.org】 function that references a registered custom property must be replaced with an equivalent token sequence, which is equal to the token sequence that would have been produced by 【111†serializing†drafts.csswg.org】 the computed value, and 【112†tokenizing†drafts.csswg.org】 the resulting string.\nL216: Suppose that --x is registered with <length> syntax, and that --y is an unregistered custom property.\nL217: \nL218:     div {\nL219:       font-size: 10px;\nL220:       --x: 8em;\nL221:       --y: var(--x);\nL222:     }\nL223: \nL224: \nL225: Because the computed value of --x (when serialized) is \"80px\", the computed value of --y is a 【113†<dimension-token>†drafts.csswg.org】 with a value of \"80\" and unit \"px\".\nL226: #### 2.7.1. Dependency Cycles via Relative Units\nL227: \nL228: 【81†Registered custom properties】 follow the same rules for dependency cycle resolution as unregistered 【77†custom properties†drafts.csswg.org】, with the following additional constraints:\nL229: \nL230: For any registered custom property with a 【114†<length>†drafts.csswg.org】 or 【115†<length-percentage>†drafts.csswg.org】 syntax component:\nL231:   * If the property contains any of the following units: 【116†em†drafts.csswg.org】, 【117†ex†drafts.csswg.org】, 【118†cap†drafts.csswg.org】, 【119†ch†drafts.csswg.org】, 【120†ic†drafts.csswg.org】, 【121†lh†drafts.csswg.org】; then add an edge between the property and the font-size of the current element.\nL232: \nL233:   * If the property contains the 【121†lh†drafts.csswg.org】 unit, add an edge between the property and the line-height of the current element.\nL234:   * If the property contains any of the following units: 【122†rem†drafts.csswg.org】, 【123†rlh†drafts.csswg.org】; then add an edge between the property and the 【124†font-size†drafts.csswg.org】' of the root element.\nL235: \nL236:   * If the property contains the 【123†rlh†drafts.csswg.org】 unit, add an edge between the property and the 【125†line-height†drafts.csswg.org】' of the root element.\nL237: For example, given this registration:\nL238: \nL239:     CSS.registerProperty({\nL240:       name: \"--my-font-size\",\nL241:       syntax: \"<length>\",\nL242:       initialValue: \"0px\",\nL243:       inherits: false\nL244:     });\nL245: \nL246: \nL247: the following will produce a dependency cycle:\nL248: \nL249:     div {\nL250:       --my-font-size: 10em;\nL251:       font-size: var(--my-font-size);\nL252:     }\nL253: \nL254: \nL255: and font-size will behave as if the value 【126†unset†drafts.csswg.org】 was specified.\nL256: ### 2.8. Shadow DOM\nL257: \nL258: Unlike many concepts in CSS (see 【127†CSS Scoping Module Level 1 § shadow-names†drafts.csswg.org】), property registrations are not scoped to a tree scope. All registrations, whether they appear in the outermost document or within a shadow tree, interact in a single global registration map for the `【78†Document†dom.spec.whatwg.org】`.\nL259: \nL260: Why can’t registrations be scoped?\nL261: There are clear use-cases for property registrations to be scoped—​a component using Shadow DOM and registering some custom properties for its own internal use probably doesn’t intend for the outer page to see the registration, since the outer page doesn’t even know the component is using that property.\nL262: However, there are also reasons to not scope the registration—​custom properties are used to pipe data into a component, and it’s useful for the outer page to be able to set such custom properties and have them syntax-checked by the registration; similarly, concepts such as a property’s initial value don’t make much sense unless the property registration exists globally, so it applies to the property even at the document’s root.\nL263: But the above just means that registration scope might be something that should be controllable, not that it should be forced to be global.\nL264: The reason registrations must be global is because elements can exist in multiple tree scopes at the same time, with styles from each tree scope intermingling and cascading together. This applies to the host element, which lives in the outer tree but is stylable from the shadow tree by the 【128†:host†drafts.csswg.org】 selector, but also elements inside a shadow DOM that are targetable from the outer tree by the 【129†::part()†drafts.csswg.org】 pseudo-element.\nL265: If registrations could be scoped to a tree scope, and a single property was registered both inside and outside, it’s not clear which registration should be applied to parse the value.\nL266: Even if we tracked which tree a value came from (something we do for other tree-scoped values) and applied the corresponding registration, it’s not clear that this would give a reasonable result—​the shadow DOM might expect a property to have a particular value space, and be surprised when it receives something completely different due to the outer tree winning the cascade and applying its own registration.\nL267: When custom properties are exposed as part of a Shadow DOM-using component’s public API, this global registration behavior works as intended. If the outer page is using a custom property of the same name for different purposes, that is already a conflict that needs to be resolved, and the registration behavior does not make it worse.\nL268: If a custom property is intended for private internal usage for a component, however, it is recommended that the property be given a likely-unique name, to minimize the possibility of a clash with any other context. This can be done, for example, by including the project name, or some short random string of text, in the name of the property.\nL269: ## 3. The @property Rule\nL270: \nL271: The 【74†@property】 rule represents a 【96†custom property registration】 directly in a stylesheet without having to run any JS. Valid 【74†@property】 rules result in a 【81†registered custom property】, as if `【73†registerProperty()】` had been called with equivalent parameters.\nL272: \nL273: The syntax of 【74†@property】 is:\nL274: \nL275:     @property 【130†<custom-property-name>†drafts.csswg.org】【131†#†drafts.csswg.org】 {\nL276:       【132†<declaration-list>†drafts.csswg.org】\nL277:     }\nL278: A valid 【74†@property】 rule represents a 【96†custom property registration】 for each 【130†<custom-property-name>†drafts.csswg.org】 in the rule’s prelude, with the property name being the serialization of that 【130†<custom-property-name>†drafts.csswg.org】 and all other fields identical.\nL279: The following rule registers three properties with the same syntax, inheritance behavior, and initial value:\nL280: \nL281:     @property --width, --height, --depth {\nL282:       syntax: \"<length>\";\nL283:       inherits: false;\nL284:       initial-value: 0px;\nL285:     }\nL286: \nL287: \nL288: While the 【130†<custom-property-name>†drafts.csswg.org】 is required, all of the descriptors are optional. When omitted, it matches the behavior of an unregistered 【77†custom property†drafts.csswg.org】.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Custom Properties for Cascading Variables Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-variables-1/)\nciteturn3070view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3066view1\",\"lineno\":408}); Total lines: 787\nL371:   * 【538†variable-declaration-08.html†wpt.fyi】 【539†(live test)†wpt.live】 【540†(source)†github.com】\nL372:   * 【541†variable-declaration-09.html†wpt.fyi】 【542†(live test)†wpt.live】 【543†(source)†github.com】\nL373:   * 【544†variable-declaration-10.html†wpt.fyi】 【545†(live test)†wpt.live】 【546†(source)†github.com】\nL374:   * 【547†variable-declaration-11.html†wpt.fyi】 【548†(live test)†wpt.live】 【549†(source)†github.com】\nL375:   * 【550†variable-declaration-12.html†wpt.fyi】 【551†(live test)†wpt.live】 【552†(source)†github.com】\nL376:   * 【553†variable-declaration-13.html†wpt.fyi】 【554†(live test)†wpt.live】 【555†(source)†github.com】\nL377:   * 【556†variable-declaration-22.html†wpt.fyi】 【557†(live test)†wpt.live】 【558†(source)†github.com】\nL378: In an exception to the usual 【277†comma elision rules】, which require commas to be omitted when they’re not separating values, a bare comma, with nothing following it, must be treated as valid in 【55†var()】, indicating an empty fallback value.\nL379: \nL380: Tests\nL381:   * 【559†variable-declaration-07.html†wpt.fyi】 【560†(live test)†wpt.live】 【561†(source)†github.com】\nL382:   * 【562†variable-declaration-37.html†wpt.fyi】 【563†(live test)†wpt.live】 【564†(source)†github.com】\nL383:   * 【565†variable-reference-06.html†wpt.fyi】 【566†(live test)†wpt.live】 【567†(source)†github.com】\nL384:   * 【568†variable-reference-11.html†wpt.fyi】 【569†(live test)†wpt.live】 【570†(source)†github.com】\nL385:   * 【571†variable-reference-26.html†wpt.fyi】 【572†(live test)†wpt.live】 【573†(source)†github.com】\nL386:   * 【574†variable-reference-27.html†wpt.fyi】 【575†(live test)†wpt.live】 【576†(source)†github.com】\nL387: Note: That is, var(--a,) is a valid function, specifying that if the --a custom property is invalid or missing, the 【55†var()】 should be replaced with nothing.\nL388: \nL389: Note: The syntax of the fallback, like that of 【54†custom properties】, allows commas. For example, var(--foo, red, blue) defines a fallback of red, blue; that is, anything between the first comma and the end of the function is considered a fallback value.\nL390: The fallback value allows for some types of defensive coding. For example, an author may create a component intended to be included in a larger application, and use variables to style it so that it’s easy for the author of the larger application to theme the component to match the rest of the app.\nL391: Without fallback, the app author must supply a value for every variable that your component uses. With fallback, the component author can supply defaults, so the app author only needs to supply values for the variables they wish to override.\nL392: \nL393:     /* In the component's style: */\nL394:     .component .header {\nL395:       color: var(--header-color, blue);\nL396:     }\nL397:     .component .text {\nL398:       color: var(--text-color, black);\nL399:     }\nL400: \nL401:     /* In the larger application's style: */\nL402:     .component {\nL403:       --text-color: #080;\nL404:       /* header-color isn't set,\nL405:          and so remains blue,\nL406:          the fallback value */\nL407:     }\nL408: To 【577†replace a var() function】, given a list of `arguments`:\nL409: \nL410:   1. Let `el` be the element that the style containing the 【55†var()】 function is being applied to. Let `first arg` be the first 【63†<declaration-value>】 in `arguments`. Let `second arg` be the <declaration-value>? passed after the comma, or null if there was no comma.\nL411:   2. 【61†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions】 in `first arg`, then 【578†parse】 it as a 【276†<custom-property-name>】. If parsing returned a <custom-property-name>, let `result` be the 【271†computed value】 of the corresponding 【54†custom property】 on `el`. Otherwise, let `result` be the 【66†guaranteed-invalid value】.\nL412: \nL413: Note: Determining the 【271†computed value】 for the 【54†custom property】 implies that 【579†property replacement】 takes place, which may cause a 【580†cycle】.\nL414:   3. If the 【54†custom property】 named by the 【55†var()】’s first argument is 【227†animation-tainted】, and the var() is being used in a property that is 【581†not animatable】, set `result` to the 【66†guaranteed-invalid value】.\nL415: \nL416:   4. If `result` contains the 【66†guaranteed-invalid value】, and `second arg` was provided, set `result` to the result of 【61†substitute arbitrary substitution functions】 on `second arg`.\nL417: \nL418:   5. Return `result`.\nL419: Due to 【579†property replacement】, 【54†custom properties】 can form 【580†cycles】:\nL420: \nL421:     :root {\nL422:       --one: calc(var(--two) + 20px);\nL423:       --two: calc(var(--one) - 20px);\nL424:     }\nL425: \nL426: \nL427: In the above, both --one and --two compute to the 【66†guaranteed-invalid value】, since their 【582†substitution contexts】 have been marked as cyclic.\nL428: \nL429: Tests\nL430:   * 【583†variable-cycles.html†wpt.fyi】 【584†(live test)†wpt.live】 【585†(source)†github.com】\nL431:   * 【586†variable-declaration-30.html†wpt.fyi】 【587†(live test)†wpt.live】 【588†(source)†github.com】\nL432:   * 【589†variable-declaration-48.html†wpt.fyi】 【590†(live test)†wpt.live】 【591†(source)†github.com】\nL433:   * 【592†variable-declaration-49.html†wpt.fyi】 【593†(live test)†wpt.live】 【594†(source)†github.com】\nL434:   * 【595†variable-declaration-50.html†wpt.fyi】 【596†(live test)†wpt.live】 【597†(source)†github.com】\nL435:   * 【598†variable-reference-39.html†wpt.fyi】 【599†(live test)†wpt.live】 【600†(source)†github.com】\nL436: Note that the 【54†custom property】 name looked up by a 【55†var()】 may itself come from a var() function:\nL437: \nL438:     :root {\nL439:       --other: 10px;\nL440:       --myvar: --other;\nL441:       --result: var(var(--myvar));\nL442:     }\nL443: \nL444: \nL445: Since the inner var() is resolved before the outer var(), the computed value of --result becomes 10px.\nL446: ## 4. APIs\nL447: \nL448: All 【54†custom property】 【601†declarations】 have the 【602†case-sensitive flag】 set.\nL449: \nL450: Tests\nL451: \nL452:   * 【603†variable-definition.html†wpt.fyi】 【604†(live test)†wpt.live】 【605†(source)†github.com】\nL453:   * 【606†variable-invalidation.html†wpt.fyi】 【607†(live test)†wpt.live】 【608†(source)†github.com】\nL454: Note: Custom properties do not appear on a CSSStyleDeclaration object in camel-cased form, because their names may have both upper and lowercase letters which indicate distinct custom properties. The sort of text transformation that automatic camel-casing performs is incompatible with this. They can still be accessed by their proper name via 【609†getPropertyValue()】/etc.\nL455: ### 4.1. Serializing Custom Properties\nL456: \nL457: Custom property names must be serialized as the exact code point sequence provided by the author, including not altering the case.\nL458: \nL459: Note: For non-custom properties, property names are restricted to the ASCII range and are 【108†ASCII case-insensitive†infra.spec.whatwg.org】, so implementations typically serialize the name lowercased.\nL460: Specified values of 【54†custom properties】 must be serialized exactly as specified by the author (with one exception, below). Simplifications that might occur in other properties, such as dropping comments, normalizing whitespace, reserializing numeric tokens from their value, etc., must not occur.\nL461: \nL462: Computed values of 【54†custom properties】 must similarly be serialized exactly as specified by the author (with the same one exception, below), save for the replacement of any 【55†var()】 functions.\nL463: The one exception to serialization being precisely as specified in the source file is that an empty custom property (such as --foo:;) must serialize with a single space as its value.\nL464: \nL465: Tests\nL466: \nL467:   * 【610†variable-reference-shorthands-cssom.html†wpt.fyi】 【611†(live test)†wpt.live】 【612†(source)†github.com】\nL468:   * 【613†variable-reference-variable.html†wpt.fyi】 【614†(live test)†wpt.live】 【615†(source)†github.com】\nL469: For example, given the following properties:\nL470: \nL471:     --y: /* baz */;\nL472:     --x: /* foo */ var(--y) /* bar */;\nL473: \nL474: \nL475: the serialization of the specified value of --x must be `\"/* foo */ var(--y) /* bar */\"`, while the serialization of the computed value of --x must be `\"/* foo */ /* baz */ /* bar */\"`.\nL476: \nL477: (Note that the leading whitespace on the value is automatically trimmed by the CSS parser; it’s not preserved here.)\nL478: This \"exactly as specified\" requirement exists because authors sometimes store non-CSS information in custom properties, and \"normalizing\" this information can change it in ways that break author code.\nL479: \nL480: For example, storing a UUID in a custom property, like --uuid: 12345678-12e3-8d9b-a456-426614174000, requires the UUID to be echoed back out as written when it’s accessed by script.\nL481: This value is technically parsed by CSS as a series of adjacent numbers and dimensions. In particular, the segment \"-12e3\" parses as a number, equal to -12000. Reserializing it in that form, as required by CSSOM in other contexts, would fatally break the author’s use of the value.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn3070view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3068view0\",\"lineno\":1738}); Total lines: 2555\nL1702: Note: The 【866†spread syntax】 is only used within an 【126†arbitrary substitution function’s】 value, as it’s only referenced by the 【171†substitution】 algorithm when parsing an arbitrary substitution function. Using it outside of that, such as in 【690†width: ...var(--sidebar-width);】, is not recognized as an early invocation; instead, the periods are just part of the property’s value, unrelated to the 【127†var()】 function, and would in this case make the width property invalid.\nL1703: (This is similar to JavaScript, where this syntax was borrowed from, where `[1, ...arr, 5]` is valid, but `var x = ...arr;` is not.)\nL1704: To substitute early-invoked functions in a sequence of 【168†component values】 `values`:\nL1705: \nL1706:   1. 【526†For each†infra.spec.whatwg.org】 【126†arbitrary substitution function】 `func` in `values` (ordered via a depth-first pre-order traversal) using the 【866†spread syntax】 that is not nested in the contents of another arbitrary substitution function:\nL1707: \nL1708:     1. 【863†Substitute early-invoked functions】 in `func`’s contents, and let `early result` be the result.\nL1709:     2. If `early result` contains the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】, replace `func` in `values` with the guaranteed-invalid value and 【527†continue†infra.spec.whatwg.org】.\nL1710: \nL1711:     3. 【160†Parse】 `early result` acccording to `func`’s 【521†argument grammar】. If this returns failure, replace `func` in values with the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】 and 【527†continue†infra.spec.whatwg.org】; otherwise, let `arguments` be the result.\nL1712:     4. 【525†Replace an arbitrary substitution function】 for `func`, given `arguments`, as defined by that function. Let `result` be the returned list of 【168†component values】.\nL1713: \nL1714:     5. If `result` contains the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】, replace `func` in `values` with the guaranteed-invalid value. Otherwise, replace `func` in `values` with `result`.\nL1715: \nL1716:   2. Return `values`.\nL1717: ### Resolving in Properties\nL1718: \nL1719: Unless otherwise specified, 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】 can be used in place of any part of any property’s value (including within other 【114†functional notations】); and are not valid in any other context.\nL1720: \nL1721: Should any of these functions be valid in contexts outside of properties?\nL1722: \nL1723: Tests\nL1724:   * 【867†variable-external-font-face-01.html†wpt.fyi】 【868†(live test)†wpt.live】 【869†(source)†github.com】\nL1725:   * 【870†variable-font-face-01.html†wpt.fyi】 【871†(live test)†wpt.live】 【872†(source)†github.com】\nL1726:   * 【873†variable-font-face-02.html†wpt.fyi】 【874†(live test)†wpt.live】 【875†(source)†github.com】\nL1727: \nL1728: For example, the following code incorrectly attempts to use a variable as a property name:\nL1729: \nL1730:     .foo {\nL1731:       --side: margin-top;\nL1732:       var(--side): 20px;\nL1733:     }\nL1734: This is not equivalent to setting 【776†margin-top: 20px;】. Instead, the second declaration is simply thrown away as a syntax error for having an invalid property name.\nL1735: \nL1736: If a property value contains one or more 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】, and all of those functions are themselves syntactically valid according to their 【521†argument grammar】s, the entire value’s grammar must be assumed to be valid at parse time.\nL1737: \nL1738: Tests\nL1739:   * 【876†variable-reference-18.html†wpt.fyi】 【877†(live test)†wpt.live】 【878†(source)†github.com】\nL1740:   * 【879†variable-reference-19.html†wpt.fyi】 【880†(live test)†wpt.live】 【881†(source)†github.com】\nL1741:   * 【882†variable-reference-30.html†wpt.fyi】 【883†(live test)†wpt.live】 【884†(source)†github.com】\nL1742: 【126†Arbitrary substitution functions】 are 【171†substituted】 during style 【173†computation】, before any other value transformations or introspection can occur. If a property, after 【531†property replacement】, does not match its declared grammar, the declaration is 【486†invalid at computed-value time】.\nL1743: Note: Since 【126†arbitrary substitution functions】 resolve at 【173†computed value】 time, if the resulting value after substitution is invalid, the property falls back (essentially) to 【885†unset】 behavior, rather than falling back to an earlier value in the 【482†cascade】 the way declarations invalid at parse time do. See 【80†Invalid Substitution】.\nL1744: \nL1745: Tests\nL1746:   * 【886†variable-declaration-16.html†wpt.fyi】 【887†(live test)†wpt.live】 【888†(source)†github.com】\nL1747:   * 【889†variable-declaration-17.html†wpt.fyi】 【890†(live test)†wpt.live】 【891†(source)†github.com】\nL1748:   * 【892†variable-declaration-18.html†wpt.fyi】 【893†(live test)†wpt.live】 【894†(source)†github.com】\nL1749:   * 【895†variable-declaration-19.html†wpt.fyi】 【896†(live test)†wpt.live】 【897†(source)†github.com】\nL1750:   * 【898†variable-declaration-21.html†wpt.fyi】 【899†(live test)†wpt.live】 【900†(source)†github.com】\nL1751:   * 【901†variable-transitions-transition-property-all-before-value.html†wpt.fyi】 【902†(live test)†wpt.live】 【903†(source)†github.com】\nL1752:   * 【904†variable-transitions-value-before-transition-property-all.html†wpt.fyi】 【905†(live test)†wpt.live】 【906†(source)†github.com】\nL1753: If a property value, after 【531†property replacement】, contains only a single 【484†CSS-wide keyword】 (and possibly whitespace/comments), its value is determined as if that keyword were its 【393†specified value】 all along.\nL1754: \nL1755: Tests\nL1756:   * 【907†whitespace-in-fallback-crash.html†wpt.fyi】 【908†(live test)†wpt.live】 【909†(source)†github.com】\nL1757:   * 【910†wide-keyword-fallback-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【911†(live test)†wpt.live】 【912†(source)†github.com】\nL1758:   * 【913†wide-keyword-fallback-002.html†wpt.fyi】 【914†(live test)†wpt.live】 【915†(source)†github.com】\nL1759: For example, the following usage is fine from a syntax standpoint, but results in nonsense when the variable is substituted in:\nL1760: \nL1761:     :root { --looks-valid: 20px; }\nL1762:     p { background-color: var(--looks-valid); }\nL1763: \nL1764: \nL1765: Since 20px is an invalid value for 【916†background-color】, the property becomes 【486†invalid at computed-value time】, and instead resolves to 【917†transparent】 (the 【918†initial value】 for background-color).\nL1766: If the property was one that’s inherited by default, such as 【919†color】, it would compute to the inherited value rather than the initial value.\nL1767: \nL1768: While a 【127†var()】 function can’t get a 【484†CSS-wide keyword】 from the 【542†custom property】 itself—​if you tried to specify that, like --foo: initial;, it would just trigger 【920†explicit defaulting】 for the custom property—​it can have a CSS-wide keyword in its fallback:\nL1769: \nL1770:     p { color: var(--does-not-exist, initial); }\nL1771: In the above code, if the --does-not-exist property didn’t exist or is 【486†invalid at computed-value time】, the 【127†var()】 will instead substitute in the 【921†initial】 keyword, making the property behave as if it was originally 【919†color: initial】. This will make it take on the document’s initial color value, rather than defaulting to inheritance, as it would if there were no fallback.\nL1772: \nL1773: To replace substitution functions in a property `prop`:\nL1774:   1. 【171†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions】 in `prop`’s value, given «\"property\", `prop`’s name» as the 【529†substitution context】. Let `result` be the returned 【168†component value】 sequence.\nL1775: \nL1776:   2. If `result` contains the 【169†guaranteed-invalid value】, `prop` is 【486†invalid at computed-value time】; return.\nL1777: \nL1778:   3. 【160†Parse】 `result` according to `prop`’s grammar. If this returns failure, `prop` is 【486†invalid at computed-value time】; return.\nL1779:   4. Otherwise, replace `prop`’s value with the parsed result.\nL1780: \nL1781: Tests\nL1782:   * 【922†css-variable-change-style-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【923†(live test)†wpt.live】 【924†(source)†github.com】\nL1783:   * 【925†css-variable-change-style-002.html†wpt.fyi】 【926†(live test)†wpt.live】 【927†(source)†github.com】\nL1784:   * 【928†variable-declaration-01.html†wpt.fyi】 【929†(live test)†wpt.live】 【930†(source)†github.com】\nL1785:   * 【931†variable-declaration-02.html†wpt.fyi】 【932†(live test)†wpt.live】 【933†(source)†github.com】\nL1786:   * 【934†variable-declaration-03.html†wpt.fyi】 【935†(live test)†wpt.live】 【936†(source)†github.com】\nL1787:   * 【937†variable-declaration-04.html†wpt.fyi】 【938†(live test)†wpt.live】 【939†(source)†github.com】\nL1788:   * 【940†variable-declaration-05.html†wpt.fyi】 【941†(live test)†wpt.live】 【942†(source)†github.com】\nL1789:   * 【943†variable-generated-content-dynamic-001.html†wpt.fyi】 【944†(live test)†wpt.live】 【945†(source)†github.com】\nL1790:   * 【946†variable-presentation-attribute.html†wpt.fyi】 【947†(live test)†wpt.live】 【948†(source)†github.com】\nL1791:   * 【949†variable-reference-01.html†wpt.fyi】 【950†(live test)†wpt.live】 【951†(source)†github.com】\nL1792:   * 【952†variable-reference-02.html†wpt.fyi】 【953†(live test)†wpt.live】 【954†(source)†github.com】\nL1793:   * 【955†variable-reference-03.html†wpt.fyi】 【956†(live test)†wpt.live】 【957†(source)†github.com】\nL1794:   * 【958†variable-reference-04.html†wpt.fyi】 【959†(live test)†wpt.live】 【960†(source)†github.com】\n"
